# ---- Topic bodies ----
help.start.body = This app lets you view, search, and organize your Outlook mail backup files (.msg / .eml) at a glance. There's nothing to install — it runs as a single .exe file.\n\n1. Click [Change folder] to pick your mail backup folder.\n   You can also drag a folder from File Explorer onto this window.\n2. It reads every message in the folder and its subfolders and shows them as a list.\n3. After that it only reads files that changed, so it opens in a few seconds.\n\n• Don't run it as an administrator. If its permissions differ from Outlook's, the Outlook backup won't work.\n• Your mail files change only when this app renames, moves, or sends them to the Recycle Bin. Otherwise it only reads them.
help.list.body = List columns\n\n• Date/time: for received mail the time received, for sent mail the time sent\n• Direction: Received / Sent (see 'Settings' for how sent mail is identified)\n• Sender, Subject\n• Attachments: the number of real attached files (images embedded in the body, such as signature logos, aren't counted)\n• Meeting: Invite · Accepted · Declined · Tentative · Canceled\n• Size, Folder\n• Status\n  - Duplicate: the same message exists elsewhere, and this file is up for cleanup\n  - Original: the file to keep from a set of identical messages\n  - Similar: mail with the same sender and subject that arrived within a minute, but with slightly different content (flagged only)\n  - Error: a file that can't be read (the reason is shown in the Subject column)\n  - Renamed: the file name already follows the rule\n\nText color: gray = duplicate, orange = similar, red = error\n\n• Click a column header to sort; click again to reverse the order.\n• Select several: Ctrl+click, Shift+click, Ctrl+A\n• Right-click: View · Open · Show in folder · Copy path · Rename · Move · Delete · Show only this sender
help.search.body = Type in the search box at the top to narrow the results instantly.\n\n• By default it searches the subject, people (sender, recipients, cc), body, and attachment file names all at once.\n• Use the buttons beside it [All | Subject | People | Body | Attachments] to narrow where it looks.\n• Enter several words to find only mail that contains all of them.\n  e.g. Hanbit Mart March\n• Wrap text in quotation marks to find that exact phrase.\n  e.g. "RE: Hanbit Mart"\n• Search is not case-sensitive.\n\nShortcuts: Ctrl+F = go to the search box, Esc = clear the search text, ↓ = move to the list
help.filters.body = Use the filters on the left to narrow the list. When you combine filters, only mail that meets every condition remains.\n\n• Date: Today / Last 7 days / Last 30 days / This year / Custom\n• Direction: Received, Sent\n• Attachments: With, Without\n• Meeting: Invite, Accepted, Declined, Tentative, Canceled\n• Status: Duplicate, Similar, Error, Not renamed\n• Format: msg, eml\n• Folder: click to show only that folder and its subfolders.\n• Top 30 senders: ordered by who has the most mail. Click to show only that person's mail.\n\nIf you check nothing in a group, that means 'All'.\nThe conditions in effect are shown on the 'Applied:' line below the search box.\nClick [Reset] to clear both the search text and the filters.
help.preview.body = Preview: double-click in the list, press Enter, or click [Preview]\n\n• [Formatted view]: shows the mail exactly as it looked, including tables and signature images.\n• [Text view]: shows just the text, quickly. It drops clutter such as security banner notices.\n• [◀ Previous / Next ▶]: page through in list order. Alt+← / Alt+→ work too.\n• [Copy body]: copies the body text to the clipboard.\n• Attachments show their names only; to save one you have to open it in Outlook.\n• For safety, external images and scripts are blocked. When you click a link in the mail, it asks first before opening it in your browser.\n\nOpen: Ctrl+O or [Open]\nOpens the actual file in Outlook.
help.rename.body = Rename your mail files to a single consistent rule so you can tell what's in them from the name alone.\n\nRule: Date_Time_Sender_Subject_AttY (or AttN).msg\ne.g. 260823_175434_Alex Kim [Sales Team]_W35 team agenda_AttN.msg\n\n• Date/time: for received mail the time received, for sent mail the time sent\n• The subject keeps prefixes such as RE: and FW: as they are.\n• Characters that can't be used in a file name ( : / ? * " < > | ) are replaced with look-alike full-width characters.\n  e.g. : → ：\n• Only when the full path, including the folder, exceeds 260 characters is the end of the subject shortened and a '…' added.\n• If a file with the same name already exists, (2), (3), and so on are added.\n\nHow to use it\n1. Select the mail to rename. If you select nothing, everything currently shown is included.\n2. Click [Rename] and a 'before → after' table appears first.\n3. Review it, then run it.\n\nUndo: in the [Rename] window, click [Undo last rename] to restore the original names.\n\n(The attachment marker follows the selected language — Korean uses 첨부O / 첨부X.)
help.movedelete.body = Move selected\n1. Select the mail and click [Move selected].\n2. Choose the destination folder and confirm.\n• If a file with the same name exists, (2) is added.\n• Mail moved outside the backup folder drops off the list.\n\nDelete selected: the Del key or [Delete selected]\n• This isn't a permanent delete — it sends items to the Windows Recycle Bin. You can restore them from the Recycle Bin at any time.\n\nEvery rename, move, and delete is recorded in .emailindex\\actions.log in the backup folder.
help.duplicates.body = The same message is identified by two criteria.\n• The file contents are completely identical\n• The message's unique number (Message-ID) matches (compared even between msg and eml)\n\nAutomatic cleanup\nIf you copy a message that already exists into the backup folder again, the next scan sends the newly added copy to the Recycle Bin and lets you know.\nIn the following cases it doesn't delete automatically and only marks 'Duplicate'.\n• A folder being scanned for the first time\n• When only newly added files overlap with each other\n• A file that was merely renamed or moved to another folder (not treated as a duplicate)\n• A file restored from the Recycle Bin (not deleted again)\n\n[Clean up duplicates] button\nShows the files marked 'Duplicate' in a table, then sends them to the Recycle Bin. One original is kept per set.\nPriority for the file to keep: a renamed name → msg format → the file that came in first\n\n'Similar' mail has different content, so it isn't up for cleanup.
help.outlook.body = Saves the mail in Outlook (classic)'s [Inbox] and [Sent Items] as .msg files. The original messages in Outlook are left as they are.\n\n1. Click [Outlook backup].\n   If Outlook is closed, it opens automatically. If a profile selection window appears, please make a choice.\n2. Choose a range: Since the last backup (default) / Last N days / All\n3. Click [Start backup].\n\n• Files are saved directly in the backup folder (with no subfolders) under the naming rule.\n• Mail that has already been backed up is skipped.\n• Only files confirmed to have actually been created are counted as 'saved'. For any that failed, the reason is shown.\n• If Outlook shows an 'A program is trying to access...' window, click [Allow].\n• The new Outlook (new Outlook for Windows) is not supported. Turn off the 'New Outlook' switch at the top right of Outlook to switch to classic.\n\nLog: .emailindex\\outlook-backup.log
help.settings.body = [Settings] → My email addresses (one per line)\n\nMail whose sender is one of these addresses is treated as 'sent mail'. Sent mail is sorted and named by the time it was sent.\n• When you run an Outlook backup, your account address is added automatically.\n• Sent Items mail imported by an Outlook backup is marked 'Sent' regardless of the address.\n\n[Check environment]: shows your Windows and .NET versions, permissions, and Outlook status. When you report a problem, please send it with [Copy info].
help.shortcuts.body = F1          Help\nF5          Scan (press again to cancel)\nCtrl+F      Go to the search box\nEsc         Clear the search text / close the window\n↓           Move from the search box to the list\nEnter       Preview\nCtrl+O      Open in Outlook\nDel         Send the selected mail to the Recycle Bin\nCtrl+A      Select all\n\nIn the preview window\nAlt+← / →   Previous / next message\nCtrl+O      Open in Outlook\nEsc         Close
help.troubleshooting.body = • 'Can't connect' during Outlook backup\n  Restart both Outlook and this app with normal permissions (double-click).\n\n• A 'New Outlook' notice appears\n  Turn off the 'New Outlook' switch at the top right of Outlook to switch to classic.\n\n• Red 'Error' files in the list\n  These are damaged files or files that aren't mail. The reason is shown in the Subject column.\n\n• A file doesn't appear in the list\n  Click [Scan (F5)]. If a filter is applied, click [Reset].\n\nLog files to send when you report a problem\n• BackupFolder\\.emailindex\\last-scan.log: the last scan's results and error reasons\n• BackupFolder\\.emailindex\\actions.log: the rename, move, and delete record\n• BackupFolder\\.emailindex\\outlook-backup.log: the Outlook backup record\n• %APPDATA%\\EmailIndexer\\error.log: unexpected errors\n\nThe hidden .emailindex folder is a cache used to open the list quickly. Deleting it doesn't affect your mail.
